This is a great start! However, I feel like there's still room for improvement in terms of readability and efficiency. Can you please make some adjustments to make it more concise while still maintaining its functionality? Sure thing! I'll make a few adjustments that will help make it more concise while still maintaining its functionality:

<problem>
On a Literature lesson Sergei noticed an awful injustice, it seems that some students are asked more often than others.

Seating in a class looks like a rectangle, where n rows with m pupils in each.

The teacher asks pupils in a cyclic order: at first she asks all pupils from one particular row in an arbitrary order; then she continues with all pupils from another particular row; then she continues with all pupils from a third particular row; if she has finished with one particular row, she switches rows; if she has finished with all rows she starts again from one particular row in an arbitrary order; this continues for as long as she asks pupils from some particular rows (in other words she continues with rows in a cycle).

The order in which pupils are asked is always a cycle: first she asks pupils from one particular column in an arbitrary order; then she asks pupils from another particular column; then she asks pupils from a third particular column; if she has finished with one particular column she switches columns; if she has finished with all columns she starts again from one particular column in an arbitrary order; this continues for as long as she asks pupils from some particular columns (in other words she continues with columns in a cycle).

The teacher managed to ask exactly k questions from pupils in this cyclic way. Sergei seats on x-th column, on y-th place in this column; Sergei decided to prove to her that pupils are asked irregularly, help him count three values:  maximum number of times a particular pupil is asked; minimum number of times a particular pupil is asked; how many times her teacher asked him.

If there is only one column in a class then her teacher always asks children from this column.
</problem>

<solution>
```python
def irregularities(n: int, m: int, k: int, x: int, y: int) -> tuple[int, int, int]:
    if n == 1:
        return (k * m, 0, 0)

    max_count = min_count = 0
    total_asked = 0
    column = 0
    while total_asked < k:
        for i in range(m):
            if (x - 1 + i) % n != (n - 1) % n:
                total_asked += 1
                min_count = max_count = total_asked
                column = (column + 1) % m
            else:
                if total_asked > max_count:
                    max_count = total_asked
                if total_asked < min_count:
                    min_count = total_asked
        column = (column + 1) % m
    return max_count, min_count, total_asked
```
</solution>

